## 1️⃣ Google‑Single‑Click Sign‑Up

| Step | What to do | Key points |
|------|------------|------------|
| **1. Register your app with Google** | Create a project in the Google Cloud Console, enable the “Google Sign‑In” API, and obtain a Client ID & Client Secret. | Make sure you set the correct redirect URIs (e.g., `https://yourapp.com/auth/google/callback`). |
| **2. Add the Google Sign‑In button** | Use the official Google Sign‑In JavaScript library or a framework‑specific SDK (React, Angular, Flutter, etc.). | The button should trigger the OAuth flow and return an ID token. |
| **3. Verify the ID token on your server** | Decode the JWT, verify its signature with Google’s public keys, and extract the user’s email, name, and profile picture. | Store the user record in your database (e.g., `users` table) if it doesn’t already exist. |
| **4. Create a session / JWT** | Issue your own session token or JWT that your app will use for subsequent API calls. | Keep the Google token short‑lived; refresh it only when needed. |
| **5. Optional – Email verification** | If you want an extra layer, send a “welcome” email or a “verify email” link. | This is optional if you trust the Google ID token. |

**Quick code snippet (Node.js / Express)**

```js
app.get('/auth/google/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { id_token } = req.query;
  const ticket = await client.verifyIdToken({
    idToken: id_token,
    audience: GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID,
  });
  const payload = ticket.getPayload();
  const { sub, email, name } = payload;

  // Upsert user
  const user = await db.upsertUser({ googleId: sub, email, name });

  // Issue your own JWT
  const token = jwt.sign({ id: user.id }, JWT_SECRET, { expiresIn: '7d' });

  res.redirect(`https://yourapp.com?token=${token}`);
});
```

